  "summary": "A hydraulic power unit (power pack) converts electrical power into hydraulic power for cylinders and motors. A motor drives a positive-displacement pump (here a submerged gear pump) that draws oil from the reservoir through a strainer and delivers it to a valve manifold. A relief valve limits maximum pressure; directional valves route oil to the actuators; an accumulator stores energy and absorbs shocks; returning oil passes through a filter and cooler back to the tank, where it settles, releases air and cools.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Package / system (positive displacement pump)",
    "configuration": "Vertical motor-pump unit on reservoir lid, submerged gear pump"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "pump": "External gear pump (vane or axial piston pumps are also common)",
    "tank_rule_note": "Reservoir volume often sized at ~3–5 × pump flow per minute (rule of thumb — verify).",
    "valves": "Relief valve + 2 solenoid directional valves (CETOP/ISO 4401 pattern)"
  },
